## Principles (criteria for findings)

The principles file below governs code in this project. Treat it as the criteria for findings: when surfacing an architecture, code-quality, test, or performance concern, cite the tier and rule it would violate (e.g., `Tier 1b (locality)`, `Tier 2.7 (premature facade)`). Tier 1 violations are blocking architecture issues; Tier 2 are decisions to make; Tier 3 only as tie-breakers when nothing higher applies. Smells (rigidity, fragility, immobility, opacity) are evidence of a tier violation, never rules on their own.

{{PRINCIPLES}}

## Hard rule: severity-gate the findings (see the cadence above)

Apply the triage from the AskUserQuestion-format cadence at the top of this
skill: **auto-resolve low-stakes findings** (pick the best default, apply it, and
log it in the run digest) and **escalate only genuine forks** — a
public/observable contract change, a destructive or hard-to-reverse call, an
un-inferable use-case/workflow assumption, or a taste call with material
downstream cost — each as its own decision brief (D<N>, ELI10, Stakes,
Recommendation, ≥2 ✅ + ≥1 ❌, Net). One genuine fork, one question, never
batched. A section with zero findings gets a *"No issues, moving on."*

This review is architecture-critical, so the escalation bar is lower than in a
routine pass: a **Tier 1 violation or a load-bearing architecture decision is
always a genuine fork**. But a mechanical or clearly-correct fix is still
auto-resolved into the digest, not turned into a question.

## Presentation: HTML triage loop (default for several findings)

When the review yields **several findings**, present them as one interactive HTML
view the user triages in the browser, instead of N serial AskUserQuestion prompts
— this is the richer, lower-context-switch path. One finding is still one
decision: each finding is its own **card**, never collapsed into a report. The
decision-brief content (D<N>, ELI10, Stakes, Recommendation, pros/cons, Net) is
exactly what fills each card's `body_md`.

**Two presentation modes, same decisions:**
- **HTML triage loop (default, ≥ ~3 findings).** Walk Step 0 + Sections 1–4 and
  **collect the genuine forks without asking inline** (auto-resolved low-stakes
  findings go straight to the digest, not the spec); assemble them into a spec (one
  section per review section, one card per finding, `default_decision: "accept"`
  on the recommended option); run the loop below; apply the returned decisions.
- **AskUserQuestion per genuine fork (fallback).** Used when the user
  declines/ignores the HTML, the server is unavailable, or there are only one or
  two escalated forks. The per-section instructions in Sections 1–4 (escalate
  genuine forks per the gate) are authoritative for this mode. Auto-resolved
  low-stakes findings never become cards or questions — they go to the digest.

Build each card's `id` as `<section>-<n>` (e.g. `architecture-1`) and **keep it
stable across re-renders** (the finding-identity contract — see the protocol
below). Use `git hash-object "$PLAN_DOC"` (or `shasum`) for `source_hash`. Apply
accept/reject deterministically; treat per-card `notes` + `global_notes` as
conversation that drives the next revision (re-render with the same ids). Record
every resolution into the plan doc's "Engineering Review Decisions" section just
as the fallback mode would — the loop changes *how decisions are collected*, not
how they're recorded.

